  "ablation_size_label": "size = 16,384 x 16,384 (4.295 GB total traffic)",
  "takeaways": [
    "**128-Bit `float4` 3:1 Vector Packet Decomposition (grayscale_direct_vec4.crisp):** Because each RGB pixel is 3 `float32`s (12 bytes), loading 1 pixel per thread (input[3*i + 0..2]) issues 3 scalar 32-bit loads at stride 3 (1,549.0 µs on H100). Grouping **4 consecutive RGB pixels (12 floats = 48 bytes) per thread** maps onto **3 consecutive 16-byte-aligned `float4` loads** (`v0` = in4[3*gid], `v1` = in4[3*gid+1], `v2` = in4[3*gid+2]) and **1 `float4` output store** (##(y0 y1 y2 y3)), cutting H100 runtime by **170 µs (−11.0%)** down to **1,379.0 µs** (**3.11 TB/s = 93.0% of H100 HBM3 peak**).",
    "**Selective Cache Hints — .nc Input Residency vs. .cs Streaming Output Stores:** Across a 32-lane warp, the 3 `float4` loads (3*gid + 0..2) are strided by 48 bytes and share the exact same twelve 128-byte L1 cache lines. Using &out (.nc without :streaming .cs) on in-vec keeps those lines resident in L1 so the +16 and +32 loads hit L1 (1,379 µs vs 1,450 µs when :streaming .cs evicts L1 lines prematurely), while applying :cache :streaming (st.global.cs.v4.f32) to out-vec prevents the 1.07 GB output write from polluting L2 cache (601.52 µs on B200, **98.1% of #1**).",
    "**Shared-Memory De-Interleaving vs. Hardware L1 MSHR Coalescing (grayscale_smem_coalesced.crisp):** Staging 768 contiguous `float4`s (12 KB) per CTA via stride-1 ld.global.nc.v2.b64 ([+0], [+4096], [+8192]) into shared memory and reading at conflict-free stride 3 (gcd(3, 32) = 1) achieved **1,380.0 ± 0.2 µs** on H100—confirming that H100 L1 MSHRs already merge the three back-to-back [+0, +16, +32] ld.global.nc.v4.b32 instructions into full 128-byte cache-line requests with zero replay penalty.",
    "**Compiler Uniformity Analysis Finding (`calculate-uniformity-state`):** In `src/analysis/core.lisp`, calls to pure tensor metadata accessors (`(length~ output)`, `(extents~ output)`, `(strides~ output)`) on a `:uniform` kernel tensor argument currently fall through to `:unknown` in `calculate-uniformity-state`, causing workgroup-uniform guards like `(when (< (* bid 1024ul) (length~ output)) (sync-workgroup))` to be rejected as potentially thread-divergent unless wrapped in `(to-workgroup-uniform ...)`."
  ],
